/-!
# Homology and ambient neighborhoods of finite cubical skeleta

Source: Hatcher, *Algebraic Topology*, Lemma 2.34, pp. 137–139; Theorem 2.35,
pp. 139–140; Appendix A.2–A.5, pp. 521–523. Bredon, *Topology and Geometry*,
I.16.2–I.16.4, pp. 56–57, supplies the radial convex-body homeomorphism used in
the boundary collars. DAG node: `topo.finite_cubical_skeleton_homology_neighborhoods_g7`.
-/

noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory

namespace HolomorphicAnosov.Cubical

/-- Source-faithful homology, deformation-neighborhood and boundary-collar properties
for the original finite coordinate cells and their literal subcomplex carriers.

Source: Hatcher, Lemma 2.34, pp. 137–139, Theorem 2.35 and cellular boundary
formula, pp. 139–141; relative quotient and wedge comparison, pp. 124–126;
coefficient change, p. 153 and §3.A, pp. 261–266; Appendix A.2–A.5, pp. 521–523.
Bredon, I.16.2–I.16.4, pp. 56–57, provides the homogeneous radial homeomorphism.

Natural-language proof: Give each original closed coordinate cell its characteristic
disk by the radial disk/cube homeomorphism and affine coordinate rescaling. Proper
faces form its attaching boundary, open cells are disjoint, and their finite closed
cover makes the characteristic weak topology exactly the original Euclidean topology.
Every subcomplex and skeleton therefore has its original finite CW structure.

Integral relative singular chains are free on simplices outside the relative space;
tensoring with the flat localization ℚ identifies them with rational chains, naturally
and compatibly with boundary. Exact scalar extension preserves cycles and boundaries,
so singular homology changes coefficients by tensoring with ℚ, also in the reduced
case. Relative homology of successive skeleta is consequently ℚ on each oriented
cell in its dimension and zero in other dimensions, by quotienting to the finite
wedge of the attaching spheres. Degree zero is the direct sum on the vertices.

The exact sequences of adjacent skeleta now give vanishing above the cell dimension
and surjectivity from the q-skeleton to degree-q homology of the whole finite complex.
Hatcher's natural exact-sequence diagram identifies degree-q singular homology with
the kernel of the cellular boundary modulo the next cellular boundaries. Inclusion
of cell bases is injective on the q-cycle kernels for two q-dimensional subcomplexes,
since neither has (q+1)-cells. This proves the required inclusion injectivity.
The outer cube is contractible, so for q>0 its q-cycle kernel is the image of the
(q+1)-cell boundary map. Those images are exactly the included oriented boundary
fundamental classes. Equivalently, the span of the entire inclusion images of each
boundary's rational H_q is the whole group: that boundary H_q is one-dimensional
and generated by either orientation class. This formulation makes no arbitrary
choice of orientation. For q=N there are no further cells, giving zero. For q=0
the ranges from the actual singleton vertex carriers generate ordinary H_0.

For neighborhoods, extend the finitely many coordinate face levels to a locally
finite ambient grid. Refining only for this purpose preserves the original skeleton
and subcomplex as literal subsets. Hatcher's Nε construction builds an open
neighborhood by taking whole cells in the subcomplex and small radial collars in
the other cells. Its shrinking argument makes those collars fit any prescribed
open neighborhood. Sliding radially to the boundary, in successive intervals over
the finitely many ambient dimensions, is a deformation fixing the subcomplex.
The maps agree on faces and are jointly continuous by characteristic quotient and
interval-product descent. First choose U for the skeleton, then W for D inside
U∩V. For empty D choose empty W; its retraction and homotopy are the empty maps.

For a (q+1)-cell boundary, normalize its active coordinate cube to [-1,1]^(q+1).
Bredon's radial body homeomorphism restricted near the boundary, multiplied by the
remaining coordinates, gives the collar
Ψ(u,a)=((1+2εa₀)u/‖u‖∞, εa′). Its inverse is
u=y/‖y‖₂, a₀=(‖y‖∞-1)/(2ε), a′=z/ε. Taking 0<ε<1/2 keeps y nonzero;
the image is open and its central section is exactly the original cell boundary.
Compactness lets ε be small enough that the whole collar lies in the prescribed O.
At a point strictly inside a q-face, a small ambient ball meets that boundary only
in its affine coordinate q-plane. The free coordinate directions and their
complement are orthogonal, giving the displayed local tangent/normal patch.
No smoothness of corners or global smooth normal bundle is asserted. An empty
subcomplex has no singular simplices in any degree, so its singular chain complex
and all its homology groups are zero.
-/
theorem finite_cubical_skeleton_homology_neighborhoods
    (N : ℕ) (hN : 1 ≤ N) (K : FiniteRectilinearSubdivision N)
    (q : ℕ) (hq : q ≤ N) (D : CubicalSubcomplex K)
    (hD : D.carrier ⊆ K.skeleton q) :
    let S := K.skeleton q
    let boundary := fun c : ClosedCoordinateCell N ↦
      {x : Fin N → ℝ | x ∈ c.carrier ∧ ∃ i : Fin N,
        c.lower i < c.upper i ∧ (x i = c.lower i ∨ x i = c.upper i)}
    let retracts := fun (A U : Set (Fin N → ℝ)) ↦
      ∃ hAU : A ⊆ U, ∃ r : C(U, A),
        (∀ a : A, r ⟨a.val, hAU a.property⟩ = a) ∧
          Nonempty ((ContinuousMap.id U).HomotopyRel
            ((ContinuousMap.inclusion hAU).comp r) {x : U | x.val ∈ A})
    Function.Injective (rationalSingularHomologyInclusion hD q) ∧
      (0 < q → ∃ hboundary : ∀ c ∈ K.cells, c.dimension = q + 1 → boundary c ⊆ S,
        Submodule.span ℚ
          (⋃ c, ⋃ hc : c ∈ K.cells, ⋃ hd : c.dimension = q + 1,
            Set.range (rationalSingularHomologyInclusion (hboundary c hc hd) q)) = ⊤) ∧
      (q = N → Subsingleton (rationalSingularHomology S q)) ∧
      (q = 0 → ∃ hvertices : ∀ c ∈ K.cells, c.dimension = 0 → c.carrier ⊆ S,
        Submodule.span ℚ
          (⋃ c, ⋃ hc : c ∈ K.cells, ⋃ hd : c.dimension = 0,
            Set.range (rationalSingularHomologyInclusion (hvertices c hc hd) 0)) = ⊤) ∧
      (∀ M : CubicalSubcomplex K,
        (∃ hM : M.skeleton q ⊆ M.carrier,
          Function.Surjective (rationalSingularHomologyInclusion hM q)) ∧
          ∀ k : ℕ, N < k → Subsingleton (rationalSingularHomology M.carrier k)) ∧
      (D.carrier = ∅ → ∀ k : ℕ, Subsingleton (rationalSingularHomology D.carrier k)) ∧
      (∀ V : Set (Fin N → ℝ), IsOpen V → D.carrier ⊆ V →
        ∃ U W : Set (Fin N → ℝ), IsOpen U ∧ IsOpen W ∧
          S ⊆ U ∧ D.carrier ⊆ W ∧ W ⊆ U ∩ V ∧ retracts S U ∧ retracts D.carrier W ∧
          (D.carrier = ∅ → W = ∅)) ∧
      (1 ≤ q → q < N → ∀ c ∈ K.cells, c.dimension = q + 1 →
        (∀ O : Set (Fin N → ℝ), IsOpen O → boundary c ⊆ O →
          ∃ V : Set (Fin N → ℝ), IsOpen V ∧ V ⊆ O ∧ boundary c ⊆ V ∧
            ∃ e : V ≃ₜ
              (Metric.sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin (q + 1))) 1 ×
                Metric.ball (0 : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin (N - q))) 1),
              e '' {x : V | x.val ∈ boundary c} =
                Set.univ ×ˢ {⟨0, Metric.mem_ball_self zero_lt_one⟩}) ∧
        ∃ J : Finset (Fin N), J.card = q ∧ ∃ p ∈ boundary c,
          ∃ δ : ℝ, 0 < δ ∧ ∀ x : Fin N → ℝ, dist x p < δ →
            (x ∈ boundary c ↔ ∀ i : Fin N, i ∉ J → x i = p i)) := by
  dsimp only
  obtain ⟨hinj, hboundary, hvertices, hsurj⟩ :=
    cubical_skeleton_homology_comparison N hN K q hq D hD
  refine ⟨hinj, hboundary, ?_, hvertices, hsurj, ?_,
    cubical_skeleton_deformation_neighborhoods N hN K q hq D hD, ?_⟩
  · intro hqN
    subst q
    exact top_skeleton_homology_subsingleton hN K
  · intro hDempty k
    rw [hDempty]
    exact ModuleCat.subsingleton_of_isZero
      (singular_homology_isZero_of_isEmpty (TopCat.of (∅ : Set (Fin N → ℝ)))
        (ModuleCat.of ℚ ℚ) k)
  · intro hqpos hqN c _ hc
    exact ⟨cubical_cell_boundary_product_collar hqpos hqN c hc,
      cell_boundary_local_flat_patch c hc⟩

end HolomorphicAnosov.Cubical

